# REPUSH-FPGA

This repository contains the Gowin IDE project for the FPGA part of the REPUSH dongle.

## Repository Layout

```
REPUSH-FPGA/
├── src/                        Verilog SoC (PicoRV32 core + CHOICE-PUF peripheral)
│   ├── top.v                   SoC top level
│   ├── puf_peripheral.v        Memory-mapped CHOICE-PUF wrapper
│   ├── picorv32.v              PicoRV32 RISC-V core (unmodified, from YosysHQ)
│   └── ...                     UART, SD-SPI, SRAM, flash controller, RNG/PLL wrappers
├── c_code/                     FPGA firmware, compiled for the PicoRV32 soft core
│   ├── main.c                  Firmware entry point + debug-shell command dispatcher
│   ├── puf.c / puf.h           CHOICE-PUF driver, LR-PUF state machine, SD persistence
│   ├── bch_puf.c / bch_puf.h   BCH fuzzy-commitment enroll/query
│   ├── conv_to_init.c          Converts a binary image into a Verilog $readmemh init file
│   └── ...                     UART, SD card, SPI flash, timers, LEDs, RNG drivers
├── monocypher-4.0.2/           Monocypher crypto library (vendored, Ed25519 + Blake2b)
├── PUF-FPGA/                   Standalone CHOICE-PUF-only prototype (VHDL, Basys2 target)
├── python_scripts/             Host-side evaluation & data-collection scripts (see Evaluation)
├── picorv32_20k.gprj           Gowin IDE project — Tang Nano 20K (primary target)
├── picorv32_9k.gprj            Gowin IDE project — Tang Nano 9K
└── README                      This file
```

## Pre-requisites:
- Gowin IDE: Follow the instructions on https://wiki.sipeed.com/hardware/en/tang/common-doc/install-the-ide.html
- Make
- GCC
- RISC-V toolchain (https://github.com/riscv-collab/riscv-gnu-toolchain)


## Compile the firmware
```sh
cd c_code
make clean
make 20k
```
## Flash the FPGA
- Open the Gowin IDE.
- File -> Open, then choose `picorv32_20k.gprj`.
- Click on the "Process" tab.
- Right click on "Place & Route" -> Click on "Clean & Rerun All".
- Click on "Programmer".
- Right click on the first entry and then choose "Configure Device".
- Change Access Mode to "External Flash Mode", then click on "Save".
- Right click on the first entry and then choose "Program/Configure" to flash the FPGA.


## Evaluation

Set up a virtual environment and install the Python dependencies used by the
`python_scripts/` tools:

```sh
cd python_scripts
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

### CHOICE PUF uniformity and reliability
The device here should be the FPGA board connected to over its USB-C port (NOT through the Arduino).
```shell
# Step 1: uniformity data
python collect_uniformity.py --port /dev/tty.usbserial-XXXX --output uniformity.csv

# Step 2: BER data (all valid challenges, 100 samples each)
python collect_ber.py --port /dev/tty.usbserial-XXXX --samples 100 --output ber.csv
# or limit to 30 challenges: add --max-challenges 30

# Step 3: full analysis + plots
python analyze_puf.py --uniformity uniformity.csv --ber ber.csv --plot
```

### LR-PUF
Enrolls the BCH fuzzy commitment scheme for a given state/challenge, then
repeatedly queries it and checks whether the reconstructed key matches the
enrolled one:
```shell
python collect_lrpuf.py --port /dev/tty.usbserial-XXXX --state 0 --challenge 0
# --queries N     change the number of repeated queries (default 500)
# --no-enroll     skip enrollment and only query an already-enrolled state
```
Output columns: `query_idx, state_idx, challenge_id, success, key_hex`.

### BCH enroll/query latency
Times how long the FPGA takes to respond to the `be`/`bq` debug commands,
measured from the host side:
```shell
python collect_perf.py --port /dev/tty.usbserial-XXXX --enroll-count 10 --query-count 50
```

### TRNG randomness (NIST SP 800-22)
Dump raw bytes from the Arduino's floating-pin entropy source, then run the
NIST statistical test suite (requires at least 125,000 bytes / 1,000,000 bits):
```shell
python collect_trng.py --port /dev/tty.usbmodem-XXXX --bytes 125000 --output trng.bin

python analyze_trng.py trng.bin
```



